      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is how it is done in nacl/box, nacl/auth and box/secretbox. I think we should stay consistent within the nacl/* family for now.\n\nI actually wrote some code that has a key type and wraps the GenerateKey, Sign and Open functions. Let me just post the result of godoc here:\n\ntype PrivateKey [64]byte\nfunc GeneratePrivateKey(rand io.Reader) (*PrivateKey, error)\nfunc (key *PrivateKey) Public() *PublicKey\n\nfunc (key *PrivateKey) SignedMessage(msg []byte) []byte\nfunc (key *PrivateKey) Signature(msg []byte) []byte\n\ntype PublicKey [32]byte\nfunc (pub *PublicKey) VerifiedMessage(sigMsg []byte) ([]byte, bool)\nfunc (pub *PublicKey) VerifySignature(sig, msg []byte) bool\n\n\nThere are a couple more that may be nice to have like:\nfunc (key *PrivateKey) AppendSignedMessage(out, msg []byte) []byte\nor methods write the output to a writer.\n\nIf we want to consider a design like this, let\u0027s do it for the whole nacl directory, after this is merged. I\u0027m happy to send a change that provides such a friendly interface at the top level of nacl, wrapping the nacl/* packages. Let me know if there is any interest in that and I\u0027ll send a proposal that we can iterate a couple of times (preferably after this is one is done).\n\nThen we could also revisit the question whether the user should provide a source of randomness for key generation.",
